{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791193080,"nonce":"ETjY99ZqbMoNq6rNhfJwKleMMg8=","message":"Follow-up: CSS-first styling that fluent methods can override, and a generated\nTailwind-style fluent vocabulary. Observed and prototyped in scratch files\n(not in the repo). The plan is not decided.\n\n\nObserved with a throwaway test (removed): an element that sets bg(red) and\nhas a class whose rule says background blue renders BLUE; rounded(3) against\nborder-radius 20px renders 20. The stylesheet pass runs after the build and\norders rules by specificity, with #id last \"so they win\". Nothing in it knows\nwhich fields the element set itself. The web does the opposite: inline style\nbeats the stylesheet.\n\n\ncn_demo's theme_bundle relies on\n`#css-overrides .cn-button--primary { border-radius: 0; }` beating a cn\ncomponent's own fluent rounded(). cn sets visual defaults fluently in many\nplaces: .bg 91 calls, .rounded 75, .border 42, .gap 37, .px 20, .py 17,\n.p 13, .opacity 8 (about 300). Flipping the pass to \"fluent wins\" would make\nevery one of those beat the app's CSS and break that theming path. So two\ntiers are not enough:\n\n  component default  \u003c  app stylesheet  \u003c  call-site fluent method\n\nwhich is CSS cascade layers (ashui has the same shape: a user-agent\nstylesheet, the author's, then the element's own writes).\n\nBlinc already carries this idea, hand-rolled per field:\nborder_radius_explicit, corner_shape_locked, incidental_align_self and\nexplicit_color, in about 59 places across blinc_layout. A per-property tier\ngeneralises them.\n\n\nEach property keeps a value PER TIER and resolves to the highest present.\nPer-tier slots matter beyond precedence: removing a stylesheet rule reveals\nthe component default underneath, instead of leaving nothing. That is the\njob base_styles snapshotting does today, and its documented failure mode is\na snapshot \"contaminated\" with hover state.\n\n- Fluent methods write the Owned tier. Component internals write Default,\n  through base(|s| s.bg_surface().rounded_md()) or, as a migration shim, a\n  scope guard that makes writes made inside a component constructor land in\n  Default. The guard is implicit state, so prefer base() for new code.\n- The CSS pass writes the Stylesheet tier through the same writer.\n- State variants are closures over the same vocabulary:\n  hover(|s| s.bg_primary()). The closure receives a refinement type with the\n  same generated methods, so the vocabulary is generated for it too (that\n  type is ElementStyle today, which is yet another hand-written copy of the\n  fluent surface).\n\nPrototype, all behaviours observed: a component default of radius 12 is\noverridden by app CSS radius 0 (theming kept); a call-site radius 3 beats\neven a LATER CSS write; padding from the call site beats the component's;\nremoving the CSS and owned values reveals 12 again; Text, with no methods of\nits own, gets the whole vocabulary and hover().\n\nConsequence to state plainly: an element with an Owned base value is not\nchanged by a CSS :hover rule, because Owned outranks the stylesheet. That is\nthe web's behaviour for inline styles, but it will surprise people. Use\nhover(..) on such an element.\n\n\nBlinc's Div already has a hand-written partial one: bg_primary, bg_surface,\nrounded_lg, p_1 to p_12 in selected steps, gap_1 to gap_12, m_1 to m_8. Gaps:\nno px_4 or pt_2 (only px(units), pt(units)), no p_0_5, no w_1_2 fractions,\nno text_sm, leading_, tracking_, ring_, outline_, border sides with widths,\nno variants.\n\nThe six token enums already exist in blinc_theme (SpacingToken with 23\nsteps, ColorToken, RadiusToken, ShadowToken, TypographyToken,\nAnimationToken). paste is already in Cargo.lock (1.0.15, pastey also).\n\nMeasured, standalone crate: 31 families (p, px, py, pt, pr, pb, pl, m and its\nsides, gap, gap_x, gap_y, w, h, size, min_w, max_w, min_h, max_h, top,\nright, bottom, left, inset, inset_x, inset_y) times the 23 spacing steps is\n713 generated methods on one blanket trait, and a debug build of that\ncompiles in 0.32s. Default trait methods are only instantiated where used.\nNot measured: the same expansion inside blinc_layout, so treat 0.3s as a\nfloor.\n\nToken methods must be REFERENCES, not snapshots. Today\nbg_primary() is self.bg(ThemeState::get().color(Primary)), a constant read\nat build time, so it does not follow a theme or scheme switch. ashui's\ntoken-bound classes do. The prototype stores Val::Token(ColorToken::Primary)\nand resolves it when drawn.\n\nString form, tw!(\"p-4 gap-2 hover:bg-primary\"): needs a proc macro, since\nmacro_rules cannot read a string literal. It should expand into the fluent\nchain at compile time and, like ashui's, fail on an unknown class naming the\nnearest known one. It should read the SAME vocabulary table as the methods.\n\n\n- Whether ThemeState offers a change signal. init() triggers a redraw\n  callback so CSS variables re-resolve; token references need something that\n  re-applies on a theme change, and I did not check that path.\n- Whether cn's own stylesheet and an app's are distinguished by origin or\n  only by order. A Default tier for component CSS needs an origin.\n- A direct dependency on paste is a policy choice. It is already compiled\n  transitively.\n- About 700 trait methods is autocomplete noise for people who do not want\n  Tailwind names.\n\n\n1. Tiers and per-tier slots behind the existing writes; CSS pass writes the\n   Stylesheet tier. Do NOT flip precedence until cn defaults are moved to\n   base() or CSS; a test that renders cn under a user stylesheet is the gate.\n2. Vocabulary macro from the token tables, on Div first.\n3. Refinement type for variants, then retire the ElementStyle copy.\n4. tw!(\"...\") over the same table.","files":null}]}